_:b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Let $\\{a_n\\}$ and $\\{b_n\\}$ be two sequences of complex numbers, and let" .
_:b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b1 .
_:b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Abel's formula is analogous to the formula for integration by parts" .
_:b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "You may use formulas (10.9) through (10.13) listed at the end of Section 10.2." .
_:b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "where x is an arbitrary real number, n is a nonnegative integer, and $\\binom{n}{k}$ is the binomial coefficient." .
_:b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "THEOREM 10.7. Assume that $a_{n} \\geq 0$ for each $n \\geq 1$ ." .
_:b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b13 .
_:b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "If the partial sums are bounded above by a number $M$ , say, then the sum of the series cannot exceed $M$ ." .
_:b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "A sequence that is not bounded is called unbounded." .
_:b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Then $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} f(x) dx$ converges if and only if there is a constant $M > 0$ such that" .
_:b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "for all $n$ , then convergence of $\\sum b_n$ implies convergence of $\\sum a_n$ ." .
_:b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b17 .
_:b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Therefore Theorem 10.8 still holds true if the inequality (10.38) is valid only for all $n \\geq N$ for some N." .
_:b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "where $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} g(x) dx$ converges. Then $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} f(x) dx$ also converges and" .
_:b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b19 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "If $f$ and $L$ are complex, we may decompose them into their real and imaginary parts" .
_:b19 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Let $\\sum a_{n}$ be a convergent series of complex terms and let $\\{b_n\\}$ be a monotonic convergent sequence of real terms." .
_:b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b20 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "It is called conditionally convergent if $\\sum a_{n}$ converges but $\\sum |a_{n}|$ diverges." .
_:b20 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b21 .
_:b21 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Riemann's argument is based on a special property of conditionally convergent series of real terms." .
_:b21 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b22 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "they developed a number of “convergence tests” that by-passed the need for an explicit knowledge of the partial sums" .
_:b22 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b23 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The phrase “convergent sequence” is used only for a sequence whose limit is finite." .
_:b23 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b24 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The sum of a convergent series is not obtained by ordinary addition but rather as the limit of the sequence of partial sums." .
_:b24 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b25 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "It was shown there that every positive real $x$ has a decimal representation of the form" .
_:b25 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Let $\\sum a_{n}$ be a series of complex terms whose partial sums form a bounded sequence." .
_:b26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b27 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "A sequence with an infinite limit is said to diverge." .
_:b27 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b28 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The former are called convergent, the latter divergent." .
_:b28 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b29 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The integral $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} g(x) dx$ is said to dominate the integral $\\int_{a}^{\\infty} f(x) dx$ ." .
_:b29 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "THEOREM 10.15. Assume $\\sum |a_n|$ converges. Then $\\sum a_{n}$ also converges, and we have" .
_:b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b30 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "When the inequality (10.38) is satisfied, we say that the series $\\sum b_{n}$ dominates the series $\\sum a_{n}$ ." .
_:b30 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b31 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "we say that the integral $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is convergent, and its value is defined to be the sum" .
_:b31 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b32 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "it defines a famous sequence whose terms are called the Fibonacci† numbers" .
_:b32 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b33 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "where $|f(n)| \\leq M$ for all $n$ , then $\\sum a_{n}$ converges if $A > 1$ and diverges if $A \\leq 1$ ." .
_:b33 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b34 .
_:b34 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Two important examples known as Raabe's test and Gauss' test are described in Exercises 16 and 17 of Section 10.16." .
_:b34 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b35 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "For every real $\\theta$ not an integer multiple of $\\pi$ , we have the identity" .
_:b35 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b36 .
_:b36 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "An important example of a divergent series with bounded partial sums is the geometric series" .
_:b36 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b37 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "It is convenient to start this series with n = 0, with the understanding that the initial term, $x^{0}$ , is equal to 1." .
_:b37 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b38 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "it is permissible to differentiate and to integrate both sides of each of the Equations (10.25) through (10.30)" .
_:b38 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b39 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Gregory's series converges for each complex $x$ with $|x| < 1$ and also for $x = \\pm 1$" .
_:b39 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "A series $\\sum a_{n}$ is called absolutely convergent if $\\sum |a_{n}|$ converges." .
_:b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b40 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "1 + \\frac {1}{2} + \\frac {1}{3} + \\dots + \\frac {1}{n} \\geq \\log (n + 1).\\tag{10.8}" .
_:b40 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b41 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "This leads to the notion of an infinite integral (also called an improper integral of the first kind)" .
_:b41 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b42 .
_:b42 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "To distinguish the integrals of Chapter 1 from improper integrals, the former are often called “proper” integrals." .
_:b42 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b43 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The function I so defined is called an improper integral of the second kind and is denoted by the symbol" .
_:b43 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b44 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "We indicate this briefly by writing $f(n)\\nearrow$" .
_:b44 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b45 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The function I defined in this way is called an infinite integral, or an improper integral of the first kind" .
_:b45 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b46 .
_:b46 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "exists and is finite. Otherwise, the integral $\\int_{a}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is said to diverge." .
_:b46 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b47 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The function value $f(n)$ is called the nth term of the sequence." .
_:b47 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b48 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "This is an example of what is known as an infinite series" .
_:b48 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b49 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The area of the shaded region is $\\int_{1}^{n + 1}x^{-1}dx = \\log (n + 1)$" .
_:b49 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "which tells us that the alternating harmonic series has the sum log 2" .
_:b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b6 .
_:b50 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "THEOREM 10.11. INTEGRAL TEST. Let $f$ be a positive decreasing function, defined for all real $x \\geq 1$ ." .
_:b50 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b51 .
_:b51 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Then both sequences $\\{s_n\\}$ and $\\{t_n\\}$ converge or both diverge." .
_:b51 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b52 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "If $\\{a_n\\}$ is a monotonic decreasing sequence with limit 0, then the alternating series $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (-1)^{n-1} a_n$ converges" .
_:b52 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b53 .
_:b53 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Note that Leibniz's rule for alternating series is merely the special case in which $x = -1$" .
_:b53 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b54 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Then $\\sum a_{n}$ converges if and only if $\\sum b_{n}$ converges." .
_:b54 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b55 .
_:b55 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "However, if $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty}a_n / b_n = 0$ , we conclude only that convergence of $\\sum b_{n}$ implies convergence of $\\sum a_{n}$ ." .
_:b55 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b56 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "THEOREM 10.25. LIMIT COMPARISON TEST. Assume both proper integrals" .
_:b56 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:b57 .
_:b57 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "then both integrals $\\int_{a}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ and $\\int_{a}^{\\infty}g(x)dx$ converge or both diverge." .
_:b57 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b58 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "we must extend the limit concept to sequences. This is done as follows." .
_:b58 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b59 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "it turns out that the logarithmic series in (10.32) is valid at the endpoint $x = +1$ as well" .
_:b59 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "The convergence of this series to the sum log 2 was shown in Section 10.17." .
_:b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b60 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "Monotonic sequences are pleasant to work with because their convergence or divergence is particularly easy to determine." .
_:b60 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:b61 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> "THEOREM 10.6. If the series $\\sum a_{n}$ converges, then its nth term tends to 0; that is," .
